      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors; I understand you need a clear, technical explanation for your Security+ exam. While the concept of salting is indeed a bit like a goblin hiding in plain sight, I will strip away the fantasy elements and focus strictly on the cryptographic mechanics and logic you need to pass.\n\nHere is the technical breakdown of why salting is necessary even when hashing is already in place:\n\n### 1. Hashing is Deterministic\nCryptographic hash functions (like SHA-256) are **deterministic**. This means that if you input the exact same data twice, you will always get the exact same output hash.\n*   **Example:** If User A and User B both have the password `password123`, and neither has a salt, their hashes will be identical.\n*   **The Problem:** An attacker who steals your database can see that two different user accounts have the exact same hash. This immediately tells them those two users share the same password.\n\n### 2. The Rainbow Table Attack\nA **rainbow table** is a pre-computed table of hashes for common passwords (or combinations of characters).\n*   **Without Salt:** If an",
